{
  "slug": "fizz-buzz",
  "title": "Fizz Buzz",
  "topic": "math-matrices",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "pattern": "simulation / divisibility branching",
  "statement": "Given a positive integer `n`, build the list of values for the numbers `1` through `n`, in increasing order. For each number use the following rule, evaluated in this order:\n\n- if it is divisible by both 3 and 5, the value is the string `\"FizzBuzz\"`;\n- otherwise, if it is divisible by 3, the value is the string `\"Fizz\"`;\n- otherwise, if it is divisible by 5, the value is the string `\"Buzz\"`;\n- otherwise, the value is the plain decimal representation of the number.\n\nReturn the resulting list, which always has exactly `n` entries.\n\nFor example, for `n = 5` the answer is `[\"1\", \"2\", \"Fizz\", \"4\", \"Buzz\"]`.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= n <= 200`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "n = 1",
      "output": "[\"1\"]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "n = 7",
      "output": "[\"1\", \"2\", \"Fizz\", \"4\", \"Buzz\", \"Fizz\", \"7\"]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(n: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Decide the order of the branches carefully: the case where both conditions hold has to be settled first.",
    "One loop over 1 to n, one string appended per number, is enough; keep the four outcomes mutually exclusive.",
    "If divisibility by a fixed number must be tested without the modulo operator, subtract the divisor repeatedly from a fresh copy of the value and see whether you land exactly on zero."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The optimal solution is one loop from 1 to n that decides divisibility with the modulo operator, testing the combined case `value % 15 == 0` before the single-divisor cases so that multiples of 15 produce `\"FizzBuzz\"`. The brute force answers the same divisibility questions without modulo: for each number it repeatedly subtracts 3, and separately 5, from a fresh copy of the value until the running remainder drops below the divisor, treating a remainder of 0 as divisible. That walk costs O(value) steps per number, so the brute force runs in O(n\u00b2) while returning an identical list.",
    "why_optimal": "The output has exactly n elements, so any correct solution performs at least \u03a9(n) work just to produce them, and the modulo version does a constant number of comparisons and one string conversion per element, reaching O(n) with no structure beyond the returned list. The quadratic cost of the subtraction variant is entirely self-inflicted arithmetic, not an inherent requirement of the problem.",
    "pitfalls": "Order of checks is the classic trap: testing divisibility by 3 first turns 15 into `\"Fizz\"` and every multiple of 15 silently loses its `\"Buzz\"`. In the subtraction variant each divisor needs its own copy of the number; reusing one mutated variable makes numbers just above a multiple of 15 come out wrong, and the loop must stop at `>= divisor` so that 3 itself lands on remainder 0. Finally, numbers that are neither divisible by 3 nor 5 must be converted to their plain decimal digits with no leading zeros and no separators, and the list must contain exactly n entries."
  },
